\title{The Element Census as the Exchange Invariant of\\ White's Quadratic Conjecture}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
White's quadratic exchange conjecture---the weakest and still-open member of a
trio of conjectures posed by White in 1980---asserts that for any matroid, two
multisets of bases with the same multiset union of ground-set elements can be
transformed into one another by iterated \emph{quadratic exchange moves}, each
move replacing two bases with two others having the same combined multiset of
elements. Equivalently, the toric ideal of the matroid basis-exchange ring is
generated by quadratic binomials. We develop an abstract combinatorial theory of
these moves on multisets of finite sets and establish, unconditionally, the
necessary direction: the \emph{total multiset union} (the element census) is an
exact invariant of the reachability relation, as are the number of bases and the
multiplicity of each individual element. We prove that the classical symmetric
exchange of matroid theory is always a legal quadratic move, identifying
quadratic moves as precisely the operations that redistribute element-ownership
among bases while fixing the global census. We package the conjecture as a
predicate over an arbitrary basis family, prove the invariant is genuinely
necessary, and verify the conjecture completely on the uniform matroid
$U_{2,4}$---the smallest instance with nontrivial content---showing its three
perfect matchings are pairwise connected by basis-preserving quadratic moves.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} matroid, basis exchange, toric ideal, quadratic
generation, symmetric exchange, uniform matroid, multiset, White's conjecture.

\section{Introduction}

Matroids abstract the notion of linear independence, retaining only the
combinatorial data of which subsets of a finite ground set are independent. Their
maximal independent subsets---the \emph{bases}---all share a common size, the
rank, and satisfy an exchange axiom generalizing the Steinitz exchange lemma.
Beyond a single basis, one studies \emph{multisets} of bases and the relations
among them, governed by the basis-exchange ring.

In 1980, White formulated three nested conjectures, increasingly weak: a
set-theoretic (strongest) statement; a \emph{symmetric-exchange} statement; and a
\emph{quadratic} statement. The symmetric variant is now known to be
\emph{false} in general. The quadratic variant---the weakest---remains
\emph{open} after more than four decades and admits a clean algebraic
reformulation: the toric ideal of the matroid is generated by quadratic
binomials.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Invariance.} The total multiset union (census) is invariant under
    quadratic moves and hence reachability; so are the number of bases and each
    element's multiplicity (Theorems~\ref{thm:onestep}--\ref{thm:invariants}).
  \item \emph{Symmetric exchange is quadratic.} Every symmetric exchange is a
    quadratic move (Theorem~\ref{thm:symmquad}), characterizing quadratic moves
    as the census-preserving redistributions.
  \item \emph{Packaging and necessity.} We express White's Part 3 as a predicate
    over an arbitrary basis family and prove the census invariant is a genuine
    precondition (Theorem~\ref{thm:necessity}).
  \item \emph{Verified instance.} In every uniform matroid, symmetric exchanges
    are basis-preserving quadratic moves (Theorem~\ref{thm:unifquad}); and the
    conjecture holds completely on $U_{2,4}$
    (Theorem~\ref{thm:u24}).
\end{itemize}
The converse of the invariance theorem---that equal census \emph{implies}
reachability---is exactly White's open conjecture and is not asserted.

\section{Preliminaries}

Let $\alpha$ be a type of ground-set elements. We write $\uplus$ for multiset
addition and, for a finite set $B$, write $B^\flat$ for its underlying multiset
of elements.

A \emph{matroid} $M$ on $\alpha$ is a nonempty family $\Bfam$ of finite subsets
(bases) of common cardinality $r$ (the rank) satisfying the exchange axiom: for
$B_1, B_2 \in \Bfam$ and $x \in B_1$ there is $y \in B_2$ with $B_1 - x + y \in
\Bfam$. Brualdi's symmetric strengthening allows $y$ so that both $B_1 - x + y$
and $B_2 - y + x$ are bases. The \emph{uniform matroid} $U_{r,n}$ has as bases
all $r$-element subsets of an $n$-set; its basis predicate is $|B| = r$.

\section{Configurations, moves, and reachability}

\begin{definition}[Configuration]
A \emph{configuration} is a multiset $\mathcal{C}$ of bases; bases may repeat.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Census]
The \emph{total multiset union} of $\mathcal{C} = \{B_1, \dots, B_m\}$ is
$\union(\mathcal{C}) = \sum_{i=1}^{m} B_i^\flat$. Equivalently, for each element
$e$ it records $\#\{i : e \in B_i\}$ with multiplicity. It obeys
$\union(\varnothing) = 0$ and $\union(B \cup \mathcal{C}) = B^\flat \uplus
\union(\mathcal{C})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Quadratic exchange move]
A move $\mathcal{C} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{D}$ replaces two bases by two
others with the same pooled elements: there exist $R$ and bases $B_1,B_2,C_1,C_2$
with $\mathcal{C} = \{B_1,B_2\} \cup R$, $\mathcal{D} = \{C_1,C_2\} \cup R$, and
$B_1^\flat \uplus B_2^\flat = C_1^\flat \uplus C_2^\flat$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Reachability]
\emph{Reachability} is the equivalence closure of $\rightsquigarrow$. For a basis
predicate $\Bfam$, a \emph{basis-preserving} move additionally requires
$\Bfam(C_1)$ and $\Bfam(C_2)$; its equivalence closure is \emph{basis-restricted
reachability}. A configuration is \emph{supported on} $\Bfam$ if all its bases
satisfy $\Bfam$.
\end{definition}

\section{The census is an exact invariant}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetry]\label{thm:symm}
If $\mathcal{C} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{D}$ then $\mathcal{D} \rightsquigarrow
\mathcal{C}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The defining data are symmetric in $(B_1,B_2)$ and $(C_1,C_2)$; the multiset
equality reverses.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One-step invariance]\label{thm:onestep}
$\mathcal{C} \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{D}$ implies $\union(\mathcal{C}) =
\union(\mathcal{D})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\union(\mathcal{C}) = B_1^\flat \uplus B_2^\flat \uplus \union(R)$ and similarly
for $\mathcal{D}$; reassociate and substitute $B_1^\flat \uplus B_2^\flat =
C_1^\flat \uplus C_2^\flat$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinality invariance]\label{thm:card}
A quadratic move preserves the number of bases; hence reachable configurations
have equally many bases.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each move replaces two bases with two bases and leaves $R$ fixed; induct along
the closure.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reachability invariants]\label{thm:invariants}
If $\mathcal{C}$ and $\mathcal{D}$ are reachable then $\union(\mathcal{C}) =
\union(\mathcal{D})$; in particular every element has equal total multiplicity in
both, and the two have equally many bases. Reachability is an equivalence
relation.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Reachability is generated by moves under reflexivity, symmetry, transitivity;
Theorem~\ref{thm:onestep} is the base case, and the invariant equality is
preserved by all three. Multiplicity preservation reads off the count of each
$e$; cardinality is Theorem~\ref{thm:card}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:invariants} is the \emph{necessary direction} of White's
conjecture, proved unconditionally: the census is the only candidate obstruction.

\section{The symmetric exchange as a quadratic move}

\begin{lemma}[Head-swap identity]\label{lem:swap}
For multisets $a,b$ and elements $x,y$, $(x \uplus a) \uplus (y \uplus b) = (y
\uplus a) \uplus (x \uplus b)$. Consequently, for bases $B_1 \ni x$, $B_2 \ni y$
with $x \notin B_2$, $y \notin B_1$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:star}
B_1^\flat \uplus B_2^\flat = (B_1 - x + y)^\flat \uplus (B_2 - y + x)^\flat.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The first identity is commutativity/associativity of $\uplus$. For
\eqref{eq:star}, write $B_1^\flat = x \uplus (B_1-x)^\flat$, $B_2^\flat = y \uplus
(B_2-y)^\flat$; since $y \notin B_1$, $x \notin B_2$, the inserted elements are
fresh, giving $(B_1-x+y)^\flat = y \uplus (B_1-x)^\flat$ and $(B_2-y+x)^\flat = x
\uplus (B_2-y)^\flat$. Apply the head-swap identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetric exchange is quadratic]\label{thm:symmquad}
For bases $B_1,B_2$, $x \in B_1 \setminus B_2$, $y \in B_2 \setminus B_1$, and
any $R$, the passage $\{B_1,B_2\} \cup R \rightsquigarrow \{B_1-x+y,\,B_2-y+x\}
\cup R$ is a quadratic exchange move.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $C_1 = B_1-x+y$, $C_2 = B_2-y+x$; the required multiset equality is
\eqref{eq:star}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Quadratic moves are exactly the operations that permute which basis owns each
element without changing the census. Every symmetric exchange is such; the
converse fails, which is why White's symmetric conjecture is false while the
quadratic one may hold.
\end{remark}

\section{Packaging White's Part 3}

\begin{definition}\label{def:white}
\emph{White's Part 3 holds for $\Bfam$} if for all configurations
$\mathcal{C},\mathcal{D}$ supported on $\Bfam$ with $\union(\mathcal{C}) =
\union(\mathcal{D})$, they are connected by basis-preserving quadratic moves.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity]\label{thm:necessity}
If $\mathcal{C}$ and $\mathcal{D}$ are connected by basis-preserving quadratic
moves, then $\union(\mathcal{C}) = \union(\mathcal{D})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A basis-preserving move is a quadratic move, so basis-restricted reachability
implies reachability; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:invariants}.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Algebraic reformulation.}
Assign $y_B$ to each basis and $x_e$ to each element, with $y_B \mapsto \prod_{e
\in B} x_e$. The kernel (the toric ideal) is spanned by binomials $y_{B_1}
y_{B_2} - y_{C_1} y_{C_2}$ indexed by quadratic moves; the multi-degree in the
$x_e$ is the census. White's Part 3 reads: \emph{the toric ideal of any matroid
is generated by quadratic binomials.}

\section{Uniform matroids: a verified instance}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unifmem}
In $U_{r,n}$, $B$ is a basis iff $|B| = r$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Single-swap cardinality]\label{lem:swapcard}
If $x \in B$ and $y \notin B$ then $|B - x + y| = |B|$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Inserting a fresh element adds one; erasing a present element subtracts one; since
$|B| > 0$, these cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform symmetric exchange is basis-preserving]\label{thm:unifquad}
In $U_{r,n}$, for bases $B_1,B_2$ of size $r$, $x \in B_1 \setminus B_2$, $y \in
B_2 \setminus B_1$, and any $R$, the move $\{B_1,B_2\} \cup R \rightsquigarrow
\{B_1-x+y,\,B_2-y+x\} \cup R$ is basis-preserving.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:star} gives the quadratic condition; Lemma~\ref{lem:swapcard}
shows each produced set has size $r$, hence is a basis by
Lemma~\ref{lem:unifmem}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
$U_{2,4}$ has exactly $\binom{4}{2} = 6$ bases.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[White's Part 3 verified on $U_{2,4}$]\label{thm:u24}
The three perfect matchings $\mathcal{M}_1 = \{\{1,2\},\{3,4\}\}$, $\mathcal{M}_2
= \{\{1,3\},\{2,4\}\}$, $\mathcal{M}_3 = \{\{1,4\},\{2,3\}\}$ share the census
(one copy of each of $1,2,3,4$) and are pairwise connected by basis-preserving
quadratic moves.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each census equals $\{1,2,3,4\}$ with unit multiplicities. For connectivity,
$\mathcal{M}_1 \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{M}_2$ pools $\{1,2\} \uplus \{3,4\} =
\{1,2,3,4\}$ and repartitions into $\{1,3\},\{2,4\}$ (both size $2$, hence bases);
likewise $\mathcal{M}_2 \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{M}_3$. Transitivity gives
$\mathcal{M}_1 \rightsquigarrow \mathcal{M}_3$.
\end{proof}

$U_{2,4}$ is the smallest matroid with genuine content: rank one is degenerate,
and $U_{2,4}$ is the first case where two distinct bases must be exchanged.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Census computation.} Accumulate the multiplicity of each element
across all bases; complexity $O(m r)$ for $m$ bases of size $r$. This computes the
sole invariant of Theorem~\ref{thm:invariants}.

\paragraph{Reachability search.} Breadth-first search over configurations, whose
neighbors are the results of legal basis-preserving quadratic moves on some pair
of bases. Since the census is invariant, the search stays within one census
bucket; the recovered component is the reachability class, and comparing it to the
bucket tests the conjecture on that instance.

\section{Applications and discussion}

The invariance results use no matroid structure and extend to discrete
polymatroids, where the census is the toric degree map. Reachability by local
quadratic moves underlies Markov-chain samplers over bases with fixed marginals,
with the census as conserved quantity and connectivity as irreducibility.

A key boundary is the failure of the symmetric variant versus the openness of the
quadratic variant: symmetric exchanges are quadratic
(Theorem~\ref{thm:symmquad}), but the reverse decomposition can fail, and the
extra latitude of quadratic moves is what keeps Part 3 alive after Part 2
collapses. Uniform matroids are tractable because they are base-orderable;
dropping this hypothesis concentrates the difficulty.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Census-completeness for base-orderable matroids.} For every
    strongly base-orderable matroid, two multisets of bases are connected by
    quadratic moves iff they share their multiset union: a base-ordering supplies
    a global bijection realizing all pairwise swaps, upgrading the local census
    invariant to a global certificate via a finite descent on disagreeing
    elements, with Theorem~\ref{thm:unifquad} as the atomic move.
  \item \textbf{Bounded exchange distance.} Two configurations of $m$ bases of
    rank $r$ sharing their census can be connected in at most $m r$ moves, via a
    monotone potential counting element-ownership mismatches, bounded by the
    total element-slots.
  \item \textbf{Symmetric-exchange deficiency is a rank-3 phenomenon.} The
    failure of the symmetric variant first occurs at rank 3; all rank-$\le 2$
    matroids satisfy the symmetric-exchange property, since in rank 2 every basis
    is a pair and a single swap always returns two pairs.
  \item \textbf{Toric grading detects the exchange components.} The connected
    components of the quadratic-exchange graph are in bijection with the fibers of
    the multiset-union (degree) map; the degree map is a complete invariant
    exactly when White's conjecture holds.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The total multiset union---the element census---is the exact invariant of
quadratic reachability, along with the number of bases and each element's
multiplicity. The symmetric exchange is always a legal quadratic move,
characterizing quadratic moves as census-preserving redistributions of ownership.
The conjecture holds completely on $U_{2,4}$. What remains is the converse: that
equal census is sufficient for connectivity.

